<h1>1. Lead-in</h1>
<section class="ex" id="t1"><div class="ex-h"><div class="ex-t">12-question discussion walk</div></div>
<div class="ex-body">
<p class="rubric">Take one card (question) below. Find a partner, ask your question, answer theirs, then move to a new partner and swap questions. Continue for 8–10 minutes, meeting as many classmates as possible.</p>
<div class="added-tag">Discussion cards (one per student)</div>
<ol class="nums">
<li>Think of the last conversation you had today. What made it succeed or fail?</li>
<li>Can two people communicate without saying a single word? Give an example.</li>
<li>Is watching a film or reading a novel a form of communication? Why, or why not?</li>
<li>Which is harder to get right: what you say, or how you say it?</li>
<li>Do you communicate differently with your parents than with your friends? How?</li>
<li>What is the most common reason people misunderstand each other, in your experience?</li>
<li>Would you rather receive bad news by phone, by message, or in person? Why?</li>
<li>Is silence ever a form of communication? Give an example.</li>
<li>Do you think animals communicate in the same way people do?</li>
<li>What is one habit that makes someone a good listener?</li>
<li>Can a photograph communicate something that words cannot?</li>
<li>Do you think communication skills can be taught, or are some people simply born better at it?</li>
</ol>
<div class="panel disc"><div class="panel-t">Whole-group follow-up</div><ul class="items">
<li>Which question produced the most disagreement between partners?</li>
<li>Did anyone hear an answer that changed their own opinion?</li>
</ul></div>
</div></section>
