<h1>3. Reading: Text 1, Model of Communication</h1>
<section class="ex" id="t3"><div class="ex-h"><div class="ex-t">Text 1 (revised, B1+/B2)</div></div>
<div class="ex-body">
<p>Our study of professional communication begins with a definition of the process itself and an overview of its key components. Communication can be defined as the exchange of information between two or more participants, generally referred to as communicators. One participant, known as the source, formulates and sends a message, while the other, the receiver, receives and interprets it. Before a message can be transmitted, the source must encode it; once it arrives, the receiver must decode it. The information that the receiver subsequently sends back to the source is known as feedback.</p>
<p>Messages may be verbal, when we speak, write, listen or read, or nonverbal, when we rely on gesture, posture, eye contact or facial expression. Even physical objects and surroundings, such as the clothes we wear, the house we live in or the car we drive, can convey meaning without a single word being spoken.</p>
<p>Every message travels through a communication channel: a vocal or auditory channel, such as speech, or a visual channel, such as gesture. Television, radio, print media and the Internet are examples of technical channels that carry messages to large audiences.</p>
<p>Communication never occurs in a vacuum: it always takes place within a context. The physical context is the actual setting in which the exchange occurs, whether an office, a lecture hall or a public park. The social-psychological context comprises the cultural background and the relationships that exist between the participants, while the temporal context refers to the time of day and the historical moment in which communication takes place. All three dimensions of context shape both the content and the form of a message.</p>
<p>Communication is generally considered effective when participants share a similar body of experience. A parent, for example, may occasionally struggle to communicate with a teenage son or daughter simply because their experiences of the world no longer coincide; in such cases, real communication depends on a conscious effort to bridge that gap.</p>
<p>Communication invariably produces some effect on those who take part in it: it can alter what we know, how we feel or how we behave. When any kind of interference, generally referred to as noise, enters the system, communication becomes less effective. Noise may be physical, as when a speech impediment or illegible handwriting obscures a message; psychological, as when personal bias or prejudice colours the way a message is interpreted; or semantic, as when a lack of shared language prevents two people from understanding each other at all.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="ex" id="t3b"><div class="ex-h"><div class="ex-t">Match the heading to the paragraph</div></div>
<div class="ex-body">
<p class="rubric">Text 1 has six paragraphs. Match each heading (a–f) to the paragraph it best summarises (1–6). Do this in pairs; each pair reports one paragraph to the class.</p>
