</ol>
<p>a. Messages that need no words · b. Communication always happens somewhere and somewhen · c. Defining the basic roles: source and receiver · d. When generations do not share the same experience · e. How a message physically travels · f. Three kinds of interference</p>
</div></section>
<section class="ex" id="t4"><div class="ex-h"><div class="ex-t">FCE Reading, Part 5 format: multiple choice</div></div>
<div class="ex-body">
<p class="rubric">For questions 1–6, choose the answer (A, B, C or D) which you think fits best according to Text 1.</p>
<ol class="nums">
<li>According to the text, feedback is best described as
<ol class="opts"><li>A the interference that reduces the accuracy of a message.</li><li>B the information that a receiver sends back to the original source.</li><li>C the process of converting an idea into a message.</li><li>D the shared background that participants bring to an exchange.</li></ol></li>
<li>The writer mentions clothes, houses and cars in order to show that
<ol class="opts"><li>A nonverbal messages can be sent without any use of language.</li><li>B technical channels are the most common form of communication.</li><li>C physical noise is unavoidable in everyday interaction.</li><li>D context always determines the meaning of a message.</li></ol></li>
<li>According to paragraph 3, television and radio are examples of
<ol class="opts"><li>A vocal or auditory channels only.</li><li>B channels that reach large audiences.</li><li>C sources of psychological noise.</li><li>D visual channels used mainly for feedback.</li></ol></li>
<li>Which of the following best summarises the writer's point about the parent and teenager?
<ol class="opts"><li>A Teenagers deliberately avoid communicating with their parents.</li><li>B Differences in experience can make communication more difficult.</li><li>C Feedback is impossible between people of different generations.</li><li>D Parents should not try to understand their children's problems.</li></ol></li>
<li>The word "invariably" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to
<ol class="opts"><li>A occasionally.</li><li>B always.</li><li>C rarely.</li><li>D unpredictably.</li></ol></li>
<li>According to the last paragraph, semantic noise occurs when
<ol class="opts"><li>A a message is interrupted by background sound.</li><li>B a listener's own opinions distort the meaning of a message.</li><li>C two participants do not share a common language.</li><li>D a speaker's handwriting is difficult to read.</li></ol></li>
</ol>
<div class="panel disc"><div class="panel-t">Discussion</div><ul class="items">
<li>Which type of noise (physical, psychological or semantic) do you think causes the most misunderstandings at university? Justify your choice.</li>
<li>Describe a real situation in which the physical, social-psychological or temporal context changed the meaning of something you said.</li>
<li>Do you agree that a message can be "perfectly clear" in one context and confusing in another? Give your own example.</li>
<li>Can you think of an object, other than clothes or a car, that "communicates" something about its owner?</li>
</ul></div>
</div></section>
<h1>4. Use of English</h1>
<section class="ex" id="t5"><div class="ex-h"><div class="ex-t">Part 1 format: multiple-choice cloze</div></div>
<div class="ex-body">
<p class="rubric">For questions 1–8, read the text and decide which answer (A, B, C or D) best fits each gap.</p>
<p class="run">Effective communication does not (1) <input class="gap" size="8"/> by chance; it results from a conscious effort on the part of everyone involved. Before sending a message, a competent communicator must (2) <input class="gap" size="8"/> into account the audience, the channel and the context in which the exchange will take place. A message that is perfectly clear in one context may (3) <input class="gap" size="8"/> complete confusion in another. For this reason, professionals are increasingly trained to (4) <input class="gap" size="8"/> attention to nonverbal cues as well as to the words themselves. Body language, tone of voice and even silence can (5) <input class="gap" size="8"/> as much information as a carefully worded sentence. Feedback, too, plays a (6) <input class="gap" size="8"/> role: without it, a source has no way of knowing whether a message has been understood correctly. When feedback is absent or delayed, small misunderstandings can quickly (7) <input class="gap" size="8"/> into serious conflicts. It is only through continuous attention to both sending and receiving that organisations can (8) <input class="gap" size="8"/> a culture of open and effective communication.</p>
<table><tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>A happen</td><td>B occur</td><td>C rise</td><td>D fall</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>A take</td><td>B keep</td><td>C hold</td><td>D put</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>A make</td><td>B cause</td><td>C do</td><td>D perform</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>A do</td><td>B pay</td><td>C make</td><td>D hold</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>A tell</td><td>B communicate</td><td>C say</td><td>D speak</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>A vital</td><td>B big</td><td>C great</td><td>D grand</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>A escalate</td><td>B raise</td><td>C rise</td><td>D increase</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>A foster</td><td>B feed</td><td>C rise</td><td>D born</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="panel disc"><div class="panel-t">Discussion</div><ul class="items">
<li>Choose two of the collocations above (for example "pay attention to" or "foster a culture of") and use each in a sentence about your own university group.</li>
</ul></div>
</div></section>
