<h1>5. Listening</h1>
<section class="ex" id="t7"><div class="ex-h"><div class="ex-t">Video: The Communication Process Explained</div></div>
<div class="ex-body">
<div class="video-box"><iframe allowfullscreen="" loading="lazy" src="https://rutube.ru/video/private/18be7d8de5905bd1991e7d353b543e21/?r=wd&p=WVIFrbhCiQtISfzIO8_6KQ" title="The Communication Process Explained"></iframe></div>
<p class="rubric"><b>Pre-listening.</b> Before you watch, predict: which of the terms from exercise 2 (source, receiver, encode, decode, feedback, channel, context, noise) do you expect the speaker to use?</p>
<p class="rubric"><b>While you watch.</b> Decide whether each statement is True or False.</p>
<ol class="nums">
<li>The video presents the model as an update of Text 1's ideas, not as an alternative theory.</li>
<li>The speaker names the sender, the message, the channel, the receiver and feedback as parts of the process.</li>
<li>According to the video, noise can only affect a message while it is inside the channel.</li>
<li>The model discussed in the video is associated with the names Shannon and Weaver.</li>
<li>The video claims that feedback is optional and rarely improves communication.</li>
<li>Encoding and decoding are both mentioned as necessary steps in the process.</li>
<li>The speaker gives at least one everyday example of the model in action.</li>
<li>The video concludes that communication is a purely one-directional process.</li>
<li>The video was originally designed as an engineering model before it was applied to human communication.</li>
<li>The speaker suggests that understanding the model can help people communicate more effectively.</li>
</ol>
<div class="panel disc"><div class="panel-t">Discussion</div><ul class="items">
<li>Does the video add anything to the model in Text 1, or does it simply repeat the same ideas in a different form?</li>
<li>Choose one example from the video and retell it in your own words to a partner, using at least three terms from exercise 2.</li>
<li>Do you find it easier to understand a new model through a written text or through a short video? Why?</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="added-tag">Post-listening mingle (one question per student, 15 questions)</div>
<p class="rubric">Each student takes a different question below. Stand up, ask your question to three different classmates in turn, and note down one interesting answer for each.</p>
<ol class="nums">
<li>What was the single most useful idea in the video, for you personally?</li>
<li>Did anything in the video contradict what you expected before you watched?</li>
<li>How would you explain "noise" to someone who has never studied communication?</li>
<li>Which part of the model do you think is hardest to control in real life: encoding, the channel, or decoding?</li>
<li>Can you think of a situation this week where feedback was missing, and what happened as a result?</li>
<li>Would the model in the video work equally well for a text message conversation? Why, or why not?</li>
<li>Which is more likely to distort a message: physical noise or psychological noise, in your experience?</li>
<li>If you had to remove one element from the model, which would you remove, and why?</li>
<li>Do you think the model applies to communication between a person and a computer?</li>
<li>What advice from the video would you give to a shy classmate?</li>
<li>How is a lecture different from the two-way communication described in the model?</li>
<li>Would the model change if the sender and receiver spoke different first languages?</li>
<li>What is one word from the video you had not met before?</li>
<li>Do you think the model is equally useful in personal relationships and in business?</li>
<li>What question would you ask the speaker if you could ask just one?</li>
</ol>
</div></section>
<h1>6. Use of English</h1>
<section class="ex" id="t8"><div class="ex-h"><div class="ex-t">Part 4 format: key word transformation</div></div>
<div class="ex-body">
<p class="rubric">Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first, using the word given. Do not change the word given. Use between two and five words.</p>
<ol class="nums">
<li>It is important to try hard to understand a different point of view.<br/><b>EFFORT</b><br/>It is important to <input class="gap" size="18"/> understand a different point of view.</li>
<li>I stopped writing formal letters when I started using email at work.<br/><b>USED</b><br/>I <input class="gap" size="18"/> formal letters before I started using email at work.</li>
<li>Maria finds it hard to give a presentation without notes.<br/><b>DIFFICULTY</b><br/>Maria <input class="gap" size="18"/> a presentation without notes.</li>
<li>The last time the team communicated by phone was in March.<br/><b>SINCE</b><br/>The team <input class="gap" size="18"/> by phone since March.</li>
<li>Communication cannot be effective if there is no feedback.<br/><b>UNLESS</b><br/>Communication cannot be effective <input class="gap" size="18"/> is provided.</li>
<li>Michael's presentation skills have improved a great deal since last year.<br/><b>BETTER</b><br/>Michael <input class="gap" size="18"/> presenting than he was last year.</li>
</ol>
<div class="panel disc"><div class="panel-t">Discussion</div><ul class="items">
<li>Rewrite item 4 so that it is true about your own group: how long has it been since you last did something as a team?</li>
</ul></div>
</div></section>
<h1>7. Speaking</h1>
<section class="ex" id="t9"><div class="ex-h"><div class="ex-t">FCE Speaking format, Parts 2–4</div></div>
<div class="ex-body">
<div class="panel"><div class="panel-t">Part 2: individual long turn (1 minute)</div><p>Look at two contrasting photographs of people communicating (for example, a face-to-face team meeting and a video call). <i>Compare the two photographs and say what you think the people are finding easy or difficult about communicating in each situation.</i></p><p class="timing">You have 1 minute to compare the photographs. Your partner then comments briefly on what you said.</p></div>
<div class="panel"><div class="panel-t">Part 3: collaborative task (2 + 1 minutes)</div><p>Here are some things that can help two people communicate well. In groups of three, talk about how useful each one is. Then decide together which three are the most important.</p><ul class="items"><li>using clear, simple language</li><li>asking questions to check understanding</li><li>paying attention to body language</li><li>choosing the right moment and place</li><li>giving honest feedback</li><li>being aware of cultural differences</li><li>keeping messages short</li><li>repeating important information</li><li>matching your tone to the situation</li><li>allowing time for the other person to respond</li></ul></div>
<div class="panel disc"><div class="panel-t">Part 4: one-question-each discussion (15 questions, one per student)</div><p class="rubric">Each student is given one question below. Prepare a 30-second answer, then form a circle: each student asks their question to the whole class and calls on one classmate to answer, who then asks their own question next.</p>
<ol class="nums">
<li>Do you think technology has made communication easier or more difficult overall?</li>
<li>Is it possible to be a good communicator in one language but not in another? Why?</li>
<li>How important is feedback in a classroom, compared with a workplace?</li>
<li>Some people say that listening is a bigger part of communication than speaking. Do you agree?</li>
<li>Should public administration officials be trained specifically in communication skills?</li>
<li>Do you think written communication (email, messages) is more or less honest than spoken communication?</li>
<li>Is it ever acceptable to communicate bad news through a subordinate rather than in person?</li>
<li>How has your own communication style changed since you started university?</li>
<li>Do you think older and younger generations communicate in fundamentally different ways?</li>
<li>What role does silence play in a negotiation or a difficult conversation?</li>
<li>Can too much feedback ever be a problem?</li>
<li>Is nonverbal communication more or less important in an online meeting than in person?</li>
<li>Do you think a good communicator is born or made?</li>
<li>How should a public servant communicate differently with citizens than with colleagues?</li>
<li>What is the biggest communication mistake you have seen a leader or manager make?</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div></section>



